      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1940, Nondalton moved from its original north shore site to the current location because wood supplies dwindled and mud flats expanded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondalton%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The name Nondalton means 'lake after lake' in Dena'ina, echoing its position along a chain of lakes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondalton%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Nondalton sits in a cold subarctic climate, drier than coastal Alaska, blending maritime and continental weather patterns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nondalton_alaska",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Subsistence hunting and fishing remain the backbone of Nondalton’s economy, sustaining families and preserving traditional practices.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondalton%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Nondalton’s local government operates through a tribal council, reflecting its Athabascan heritage and community decision‑making.",
